"<p><strong>Nitrobase</strong> is a self-hosted Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) and Backend-as-a-Service (BaaS) control panel built for developers and teams who want click-to-deploy convenience on their own servers—without Kubernetes complexity or vendor lock-in.</p>\n<p>Connect any Ubuntu or Debian VPS over SSH—or manage the machine Nitrobase runs on (<em>Localhost</em>)—and let Nitrobase set up Docker and Traefik v3. From there, deploy <strong>Supabase</strong>, <strong>Appwrite</strong>, <strong>PocketBase</strong>, <strong>standalone databases</strong> (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB, Valkey), <strong>n8n</strong>, <strong>Plausible</strong>, <strong>Umami</strong>, <strong>RustFS</strong>, custom <code>docker-compose.yml</code> stacks, or your own <strong>Git repositories</strong> with HTTPS, backups, monitoring, updates, and AI-agent MCP support built in.</p>\n<h2>Key Highlights</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bring any VPS:</strong> Add remote servers over SSH or bootstrap <em>Localhost</em>. Guided bootstrap previews every change before touching the host, and ownership markers prevent two panels from colliding over one server.</li>\n<li><strong>Backends, not just containers:</strong> First-class templates for Supabase, Appwrite, and PocketBase with generated secrets, credentials panels, and app-aware database + volume backups.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe by default:</strong> Every compose file—whether from a marketplace template or your own custom compose—passes through the same security policy (no host mounts outside the app folder, no privileged containers, no reserved labels or shared networks).</li>\n<li><strong>Operations included:</strong> Automated backups to S3, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, or a local server folder with verified restores; live server/container monitoring and alerts; one-click updates with automatic rollback; moving apps between servers; a web terminal and database consoles.</li>\n<li><strong>Automation &#x26; AI Agents:</strong> Team-scoped REST API (<code>/api/v1</code>) with OpenAPI 3.1 and a stateless <strong>MCP server</strong> (<code>/api/mcp</code>) so AI agents like Claude Code and Cursor can inspect and operate your infrastructure safely.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>System Requirements</h2>\n<p>The Nitrobase panel and its PostgreSQL database use roughly <strong>300 MB of RAM and 1 GB of disk</strong>. The remaining capacity is for the apps you deploy (for example, a full Supabase stack typically uses 1–1.5 GB of RAM).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operating System:</strong> Ubuntu or Debian (<code>amd64</code> or <code>arm64</code>) for host bootstrap and the VPS install script. Any OS with Docker works with the Docker installer image.</li>\n<li><strong>RAM:</strong> <strong>2 GB recommended</strong> (the installer warns below ~1.8 GB and continues). On small VPS instances, adding a 2 GB swap file prevents OOM kills during builds or spikes.</li>\n<li><strong>Disk:</strong> <strong>5 GB free recommended</strong> (the installer warns below 5 GB and refuses below 2 GB). Use <code>--skip-resource-check</code> on small test machines where you know what fits.</li>\n<li><strong>One install per machine:</strong> The containers (<code>nitrobase_app</code>, <code>nitrobase_postgres</code>) and volumes (<code>nitrobase_postgres_data</code>, <code>nitrobase_data</code>) use fixed names. The installer refuses to start a second install on the same host.</li>\n<li><strong>Ports (Standard Mode):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><code>22</code> (SSH)</li>\n<li><code>80</code> &#x26; <code>443</code> (HTTP/HTTPS via Traefik v3)</li>\n<li><code>3000</code> (Nitrobase Web Panel; bind to <code>127.0.0.1:3000</code> once behind HTTPS)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"docCallout docCallout-tip\"><div class=\"docCalloutHeader\"><span class=\"docCalloutBadge\">TIP</span><span class=\"docCalloutTitle\">Tunnel-Only Advantage</span></div><div class=\"docCalloutContent\">\n<p>When publishing apps through <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong> in <strong>Tunnel only</strong> mode, ports <code>80</code> and <code>443</code> can remain completely closed on your server firewall.</p>\n</div></div>\n<h2>License</h2>\n<p>Nitrobase is <strong>fair source</strong>, released under the <strong>Functional Source License, Version 1.1, ALv2 Future License (<code>FSL-1.1-ALv2</code>)</strong>. You may use, copy, modify, and self-host Nitrobase for any purpose—including inside your company and for clients—except to offer a competing product or service. Each release automatically converts to <strong>Apache License 2.0</strong> two years after publication.</p>"